    "text": "To solve the problem, we start by noting that the roots of the quadratic equation \\(x^2 + bx + 48 = 0\\) are in the ratio of 3 to 1. Let's denote the roots by \\(3k\\) and \\(k\\). According to Vieta's formulas, for a quadratic equation \\(x^2 + bx + c = 0\\), the sum of the roots is \\(-b\\) and the product of the roots is \\(c\\). Here, the product of the roots is 48, so we have:\n\n\\[\n3k \\cdot k = 48 \\implies 3k^2 = 48 \\implies k^2 = 16 \\implies k = 4 \\text{ or } k = -4\n\\]\n\nWe will consider both cases for \\(k\\).\n\n**Case 1: \\(k = 4\\)**\n\nThe roots are \\(3k = 12\\) and \\(k = 4\\). The sum of the roots is:\n\n\\[\n12 + 4 = 16 \\implies -b = 16 \\implies b = -16\n\\]\n\n**Case 2: \\(k = -4\\)**\n\nThe roots are \\(3k = -12\\) and \\(k = -4\\). The sum of the roots is:\n\n\\[\n-12 + (-4) = -16 \\implies -b = -16 \\implies b = 16\n\\]\n\nAmong the possible values of \\(b\\) from the two cases, the largest possible value is \\(16\\). Therefore, the largest possible value of \\(b\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{16}\n\\]",
